.TH PAM_PEEKIN 8 2026-09-25 "PeekIn 1.0.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
pam_peekin \- PAM module for face authentication
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auth [success=done default=ignore] pam_peekin.so
.RI [ option ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pam_peekin
recognises the user's face with the camera.
It runs
.I /usr/libexec/peekin/peekin-compare
as the unprivileged
.B peekin
account, with a hard time limit, and reports success only when that worker
matched an enrolled face of the user (and, when required, the user followed
a random sequence of head turns).
In every other case it returns without success, so the next module, normally
the password, decides.
.PP
Before scanning it checks, without opening the camera: whether face login is
enabled for this service
.RB ( [services]
in
.BR peekin-config (5)),
SSH sessions and a closed lid when those guards are on, a camera being
present, the face model being readable, and whether face login is paused
after too many failed attempts.
Messages to the user (the head-turn instructions, failures) go through the
PAM conversation, in the locale of the calling program.
Diagnostics go to the system log.
.PP
After
.B max_failed_attempts
rejected faces in a row, face login pauses for 15 minutes or until the user
authenticates with the password; the module's
.B setcred
function clears the counter then.
.PP
When the worker reports a dark scene from a colour camera and
.B brighten_screen
is on, the module raises the built-in screen's backlight for the rest of the
scan and restores it afterwards.
.PP
On Fedora the package adds the module to the authselect profile, or to
.I /etc/pam.d/sudo
and
.I /etc/pam.d/gdm-password
directly, through
.IR /usr/libexec/peekin/system-setup .
.SH OPTIONS
These exist for tests and unusual layouts; a normal installation needs none.
.TP
.BI config_dir= dir
Configuration directory (default
.IR /etc/peekin ).
.TP
.BI data_dir= dir
Data directory with the recognition models (default
.IR /usr/share/peekin ).
.TP
.BI compare= path
The recognition worker (default
.IR /usr/libexec/peekin/peekin-compare ).
.TP
.BI state_dir= dir
Failed-attempt counters (default
.IR /var/lib/peekin/failures ).
.TP
.BI backlight_dir= dir
Backlights to raise in the dark (default
.IR /sys/class/backlight ).
.TP
.BI activity_dir= dir
History of attempts (default
.IR /var/lib/peekin-activity ).
.SH RETURN VALUES
.TP
.B PAM_SUCCESS
The face of the user was recognised.
.TP
.B PAM_AUTH_ERR
The user was not accepted: wrong or no face, failed head movement, time
limit, no face model, or a camera or worker failure during the scan.
.TP
.B PAM_AUTHINFO_UNAVAIL
Face authentication could not or should not be used here: disabled, switched
off for this service, no camera, paused after failed attempts, invalid
security settings, or the challenge could not be shown.
.TP
.B PAM_USER_UNKNOWN
The user name is not a valid account name.
.TP
.B PAM_SYSTEM_ERR
The configuration file could not be read.
.PP
.B pam_sm_setcred
returns
.B PAM_IGNORE
(it only clears the failed-attempt counter).
.SH FILES
.I /etc/peekin/config.ini
.br
.I /var/lib/peekin/failures/
.br
.I /var/lib/peekin-activity/
\- one file per user with the latest 200 attempts (time, service, result,
matching face model), root-owned and readable by that user through an ACL.
.SH NOTES
SELinux: the package's
.B peekin
policy module lets the login and lock screen (xdm_t), the text console
(local_login_t) and the polkit helper (policykit_auth_t) use the camera and
the failed-attempt counters.
Raising the backlight is possible from the login and lock screens and sudo,
not from the text console or polkit dialogs.
.PP
Journal:
.B journalctl \-b | grep pam_peekin
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR peekin (1),
.BR peekin-config (5),
.BR pam (8),
.BR authselect (8)
